  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 140 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nSaturday night through Thursday:\n\nThe aforementioned cold front (bisecting WI Saturday evening) \nphases well with an approaching trough in the jet, allowing it \nto feature a deepening surface pressure trough and strong low-\nlevel convergence. Both features track east, with the front \ncrossing southern WI Saturday night into Sunday morning. A \nwidespread wetting rainfall is expected through said period, \ninitial rain estimates are between a tenth of an inch and half \nan inch. With such strong synoptic forcing for ascent with this \nfront and PWAT building to just over 1\", I wouldn't be too \nsurprised to see localized areas (particularly north and west of\nMadison) approach 0.5-1.0\" of rain, but with the antecedent dry\nconditions, there are no concerns for flooding. From an \ninstability standpoint, a handful of ECMWF ensemble members \nallow 100-300 joules of MUCAPE to push into southern WI ahead of\nthe front. We're not expecting any strong storms, and the odds \nfor thunder are currently < 15%, but with such strong synoptic \nforcing we cannot rule out a few rumbles of thunder. \n\nA sharp cooling trend is expected into early next week behind\nthe front. Highs in the upper 30s to low 40s and lows in the \nlow 20s (locally warmer by Lake MI) are expected from Monday to\nWednesday. Dry weather is expected through much of this period\nas well, with high pressure building into the Great Lakes\nregion. \n\nSheppard",
